7] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "80e5b3fd-f8d6-55a8-9a84-9bc4da788fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316950 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab88447e-79ce-5c0e-83ae-9cdc9dc98f0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317044 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317044 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317044 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317044 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d350858b-5387-5d1a-9e40-018edc34bd55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317129 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6fd87bc-7a3e-5c6b-a51d-15025091ecf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317419 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $0.6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b200d20b-8850-5ea9-88a8-3f31357f34c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317419 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.6{y}^{″}=-ky-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{3}y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{3}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317420 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c255544-1d5d-5734-8a75-e3f42b3984e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317420 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=5\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317427 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c611f45-045c-5502-9311-b6daf9203ddc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317427 #edb/ref 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317430 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by 